Notebook[{
Cell[TextData[{
  "Week 6   Projective Geometry\n",
  StyleBox["Projective Transformations",
    FontSize->18]
}], "Title"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["\[Copyright] Dana Scott 1993/1997/1998",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["\nNotebook  last edited: ",
    FontSize->16],
  StyleBox["25 September 1998",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Subtitle",
  FontSize->18],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Representing projective transformations", "Section"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Making a definition", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Passing from 1 to 2 dimensions", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In discussing projective transformations between ",
  StyleBox["ranges",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (or between ",
  StyleBox["pencils",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "), we came to the following conclusion:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Theorem.",
  StyleBox[
  "  The following properties of a one-one correspondence between the points \
on one line and the points on another line (or between the lines in one \
pencil and the lines in another pencil or between the points on a line and \
the lines in a pencil) are ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["equivalent",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[":\n\t",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "(i)",
  StyleBox["   the correspondence is a ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["composition of central projections",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[";\n\t",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "(ii)",
  StyleBox["  the correspondence ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["preserves crossratios",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[";\n\t",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "(iii)",
  StyleBox[" the correspondence is represented by a ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox[
  "(non-singular) linear \t\t\t       transformation of relative homogeneous \
coordinates",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In other words, in the one-dimensional case there are ",
  StyleBox["three",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " quite different, equivalent characterizations of ",
  StyleBox["projective",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["transformations",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If we set up a presentation of ",
  StyleBox["higher-dimensional projective space",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", we could likewise say that a projective transformation between ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "-dimensional projective spaces is a composition of central projections in \
a still ",
  StyleBox["higher-dimensional",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " space.  However, the success in using crossratios makes it unnecessary to \
to invoke higher dimensions.  We can use the following as a definition:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Definition. ",
  StyleBox["A ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["projective (point) transformation ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["of the ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "2",
  StyleBox["-dimensional projective plane is a ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["one-one correspondence",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" between projective points such that \n\t",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "(i)",
  StyleBox["   ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["collinear",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" points are mapped to ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["collinear",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" points; and\n\t",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "(ii)",
  StyleBox["  the ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["crossratio",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" of four collinear points is always",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox[" preserved",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  CellFrame->False,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell["And there is a dual definition.", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Definition. ",
  StyleBox["A ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["projective (line) transformation ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["of the ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "2",
  StyleBox["-dimensional projective plane is a ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["one-one correspondence",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" between projective lines such that \n\t",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "(i)",
  StyleBox["   ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["concurrent",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" lines are mapped to ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["concurrent",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" lines; and\n\t",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "(ii)",
  StyleBox["  the ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["crossratio",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" of four concurrent lines is always",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox[" preserved",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  CellFrame->False,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The first question to ask here is: ",
  StyleBox[" Are the two definitions above unrelated?  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "or",
  StyleBox[" Do they somehow come to the same thing?",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Another good question is:",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[
  " Are projective transformations of the plane under this new definition \
represented by linear transformations of coordinates?",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It is clear, byt the way, that a ",
  StyleBox["non-singular linear transformation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " always does represent a projective transformation, because it will be \
one-one on points and preserves crossratios by exactly the same proof we gave \
earlier for transformations between lines."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Relating the point and line versions", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["We can answer the first question directly.", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Theorem.",
  StyleBox["  Every projective ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["point",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" transformation determines a projective ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["line",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" transformation, and every projective ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["line",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" transformation determines a projective ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["point",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[
  " transformation; moreover, passing from one type of transformation to the \
other and back again returns us to the same transformation.",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Proof.",
  StyleBox["  Let ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["T", "InlineInput"],
  StyleBox[
  " be a projective point transformation.  A obvious mapping between lines is \
dertermined by taking a range of points on a given line to the (unique) line \
determined  by their ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["images",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[".  Let us call the corresponding line transformation ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["T*", "InlineInput"],
  StyleBox[".  Is this transformation ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["one-one",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["between all lines?  Is it projective?",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "First, we note that ",
  StyleBox["T", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "will map the set of points on any line ",
  StyleBox["l", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["onto",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the whole set of points lying on the image line ",
  StyleBox["T*[l]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  To see this, take ",
  StyleBox["distinct",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points ",
  StyleBox["P", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["Q", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["R", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " on the line ",
  StyleBox["l", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Then ",
  StyleBox["T[P]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["T[Q]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["T[R]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " must be distinct points on ",
  StyleBox["T*[l]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Take a fourth point ",
  StyleBox["S", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " on ",
  StyleBox["l", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  It corresponds ",
  StyleBox["uniquely",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to a crossratio ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(s == RP[P, Q, R, S]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Since ",
  StyleBox["T",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " preserves collinearity ",
  StyleBox["and",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "crossratios, it follows that ",
  StyleBox["T[S]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is on ",
  StyleBox["T*[l]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "and that we also have the same crossratio: ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(s == RP[T[P], T[Q], T[R], T[S]]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  This means that the image points realize ",
  StyleBox["all",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the possible crossratios there could ever be on the image line ",
  StyleBox["T*[l]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and so they must map onto ",
  StyleBox["all",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the points of ",
  StyleBox["T*[l]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, if ",
  StyleBox["l", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["m", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " are two ",
  StyleBox["distinct",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " lines, then there is a ",
  StyleBox["unique",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "point ",
  StyleBox["P", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " at the intersection of the two ranges.  By what we just proved, ",
  StyleBox["T*[l]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is the line dertermined by the images ",
  StyleBox["T[X]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " of all the points ",
  StyleBox["X", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " on ",
  StyleBox["l", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  and consists exactly of  ",
  StyleBox["all",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the points in the image ",
  StyleBox["l", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Simillarly for ",
  StyleBox["m", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Thus, ",
  StyleBox["T[P]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " must be the ",
  StyleBox["unique",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " point at the intersection of the two ranges ",
  StyleBox["T*[l]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["T*[m]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  So the two image ranges are ",
  StyleBox["distinct",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that this also proves that if ",
  StyleBox["T", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is a projective point transformation, and ",
  StyleBox["P", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["Q", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["R", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " are three points, then the points ",
  StyleBox["P", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["Q", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["R", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "are collinear ",
  StyleBox["if, and only if",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", the image points ",
  StyleBox["T[P]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["T[Q]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["T[R]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " are collinear.  Because, if they are not collinear, then there are \
distinct lines passing through ",
  StyleBox["P", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["Q", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and through ",
  StyleBox["P", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["R", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Since the image lines are distinct, ",
  StyleBox["T[P]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["T[Q]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["T[R]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " cannot be collinear."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, using the same argument, if ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is a third distinct line concurrent with given lines ",
  StyleBox["l", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["m", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " (say, at ",
  StyleBox["P", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "), then ",
  StyleBox["T*[n]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is concurrent with the lines ",
  StyleBox["T*[l]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["T*[m]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " (at ",
  StyleBox["T[P]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ").  And, if the three lines are not concurrent, they intersect in pairs at \
three ",
  StyleBox["distinct",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", non-collinear points, so their images are ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " concurrent.  This proves that the lines ",
  StyleBox["l", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["m", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "are ",
  StyleBox["concurrent",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["if, and only if,",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["T*[l]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["T*[m]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["T*[n]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " are ",
  StyleBox["concurrent",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        SuperscriptBox["l", "\[Prime]",
          MultilineFunction->None],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is any arbitrarily given line in the plane, pick two points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        SuperscriptBox["P", "\[Prime]",
          MultilineFunction->None],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        SuperscriptBox["Q", "\[Prime]",
          MultilineFunction->None],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on it.  They must be images of points ",
  StyleBox["P", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["Q", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "under ",
  StyleBox["T", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Thus the line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        SuperscriptBox["l", "\[Prime]",
          MultilineFunction->None],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is the image of the line ",
  StyleBox["l", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " joining ",
  StyleBox["P", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["Q", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " under ",
  StyleBox["T*", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  We can conclude that ",
  StyleBox["T*", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is indeed a one-one correspondence between all lines, and that it takes \
concurrent lines (non-concurrent lines) to concurrent lines (respectively, \
non-concurrent lines).  What about ",
  StyleBox["crossratios",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "?"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We are assuming that ",
  StyleBox["T", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["preserves crossratios ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "of collinear points, and we have to argue that ",
  StyleBox["T*", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " preserves crossratios of lines as well.  Suppose that ",
  StyleBox["k", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["l", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["m", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["n", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " are four lines in a pencil making a certain crossratio ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a == RL[k, l, m, n]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Take a line ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in the pencil, and intersect the four lines in points  ",
  StyleBox["K", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["L", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["M", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["N", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Then, as we know by the Transfer Theorem, we have the ",
  StyleBox["same crossratio",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", thus, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a == RP[K, L, M, N]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Now the transformations ",
  StyleBox["T*", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["T", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " will map this configuration to lines  ",
  StyleBox["T*[k]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["T*[l]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["T*[m]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["T*[n]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "in a pencil and to a quadruple of collinear points ",
  StyleBox["T[K]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["T[L]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["T[M]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["T[N]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Because ",
  StyleBox["T", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " preserves crossratios, we see that ",
  StyleBox["T*", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " (as a line transformation) also preserves crossratios."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By a ",
  StyleBox["dual argument",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", if we start with a projective line transformation ",
  StyleBox["T*",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", then a projective point transformation ",
  StyleBox["T ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is determined by seeing where pencils go.  And if we start with ",
  StyleBox["T",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", determine ",
  StyleBox["T*",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and then ",
  StyleBox["redetermine",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " a point transformation, it must be the same as ",
  StyleBox["T ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\[LongDash]",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "just by following pencils.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Proving linearity", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
Now for the second question.  It takes a little work to relate \
coordinates along various lines to coordinates in the plane.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Every projective transformation of the projective plane",
  StyleBox[" is represented by a ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["(non-singular) linear transformation of homogeneous coordinates",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Let the transformation be ",
  StyleBox["T", "InlineInput"],
  ".  Consider the points represented by ",
  StyleBox["u", "InlineInput"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["w", "InlineInput"],
  " and the other points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ u + c\ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis.  These points are transformed to collinear points on a transformed \
line.  We thus have a projective transformation between two lines, and we \
know it can be represented by a linear transformation.  This means that by a \
careful choice of representatives we can think that under ",
  StyleBox["T", "InlineInput"],
  " we have the correspondences\n \t\t ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u \[Rule] A, w \[Rule] C, a\ u + c\ w \[Rule] a\ A + c\ C\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Consider next the points represented by ",
  StyleBox["v", "InlineInput"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["w", "InlineInput"],
  " and the other points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(b\ v + c\ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on the ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis.  These points are transformed to collinear points on a transformed \
line.  We thus have a projective transformation between two different lines, \
and we know it can be represented by a linear transformation.  This means \
that by a careful choice of representatives we can think that under ",
  StyleBox["T", "InlineInput"],
  " we have the correspondences\n \t\t ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"v", "\[Rule]", 
            SuperscriptBox["B", "\[Prime]",
              MultilineFunction->None]}], ",", 
          RowBox[{"w", "\[Rule]", 
            SuperscriptBox["C", "\[Prime]",
              MultilineFunction->None]}], ",", 
          RowBox[{\(b\ v + c\ w\), "\[Rule]", 
            RowBox[{
              RowBox[{"b", " ", 
                SuperscriptBox["B", "\[Prime]",
                  MultilineFunction->None]}], "+", 
              RowBox[{"c", " ", 
                SuperscriptBox["C", "\[Prime]",
                  MultilineFunction->None]}]}]}]}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".\nNote that by recalling the theorem concerning lines and linear \
transformation, we ",
  StyleBox["cannot",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " say at once that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"C", "==", 
          SuperscriptBox["C", "\[Prime]",
            MultilineFunction->None]}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on account of the previous result."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "However, since we are talking of a single transformation ",
  StyleBox["T", "InlineInput"],
  " of the whole plane, then ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["C",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        SuperscriptBox["C", "\[Prime]",
          MultilineFunction->None],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "have to represent the ",
  StyleBox["same point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Therefore, we have a ",
  StyleBox["dependency",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and we can write ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox["C",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{"k", " ", 
            SuperscriptBox["C", "\[Prime]",
              MultilineFunction->None]}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  " for some particular scalar ",
  StyleBox["k", "InlineInput"],
  ".  Next let us define ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"B", "==", 
          RowBox[{"k", " ", 
            SuperscriptBox["B", "\[Prime]",
              MultilineFunction->None]}]}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  So, if we replace the linear transformation between the ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis and the image line by ",
  StyleBox["k", "InlineInput"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["times",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " that transformation, we can think that under ",
  StyleBox["T", "InlineInput"],
  " we have the correspondences\n \t\t ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(v \[Rule] B, w \[Rule] C, b\ v + c\ w \[Rule] b\ B + c\ C\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let us next introduce coordinates for  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["A",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["B", "InlineInput"],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["C",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " as follows ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(A == \(a\_1\) u + \(b\_1\) v + \(c\_1\) w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(B == \(a\_2\) u + \(b\_2\) v + \(c\_2\) w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(C == \(a\_3\) u + \(b\_3\) v + \(c\_3\) w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, any point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ u + b\ v + c\ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " in the plane not on the line ",
  StyleBox["z", "InlineInput"],
  " can be captured as the ",
  StyleBox["intersection",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of two lines parallel to the axes, according to this calculation:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[a, b, c, Subscript]; 
    PE[PE[v, a\ u + c\ w], PE[u, b\ v + c\ w]] // Factor\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(-c\)\ \((a\ u + b\ v + c\ w)\)\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "(We had to assume ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c \[NotEqual] 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " here to make sure there ",
  StyleBox["was",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " an intersection.)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "But, under the transformation ",
  StyleBox["T,", "InlineInput"],
  " we know ",
  StyleBox["which forms",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " represent the images of those four points we see in this calculation.  So \
the point represented by the ",
  StyleBox["result",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the calculation must transform to the point represented by the result \
of the following calculation:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Collect[#, {a, b, c}]&\)/@
      \((Tidy/@Factor[
            Release[Hold[PE[PE[v, a\ u + c\ w], PE[u, b\ v + c\ w]]] /. \n
                \t\t\t\t{u \[Rule] \((a\_1\ u + b\_1\ v + c\_1\ w)\), \n
                  \t\t\t\t\tv \[Rule] \((a\_2\ u + b\_2\ v + c\_2\ w)\), \n
                  \t\t\t\t\tw \[Rule] \((a\_3\ u + b\_3\ v + c\_3\ w)\)}]])
        \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(-c\)\ 
      \((\(-a\_3\)\ b\_2\ c\_1 + a\_2\ b\_3\ c\_1 + a\_3\ b\_1\ c\_2 - 
          a\_1\ b\_3\ c\_2 - a\_2\ b\_1\ c\_3 + a\_1\ b\_2\ c\_3)\)\ 
      \((a\ \((u\ a\_1 + v\ b\_1 + w\ c\_1)\) + 
          b\ \((u\ a\_2 + v\ b\_2 + w\ c\_2)\) + 
          c\ \((u\ a\_3 + v\ b\_3 + w\ c\_3)\))\)\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Note. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " The ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " commands ",
  StyleBox["Hold", "InlineInput"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["Release", "InlineInput"],
  " had to be employed above so that the computation by ",
  StyleBox["PE", "InlineInput"],
  " would be done ",
  StyleBox["after",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the indicated substitution. "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This shows that the point represented by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ u + b\ v + c\ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " transforms under ",
  StyleBox["T", "InlineInput"],
  " to a point represented by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ A + b\ B + c\ C\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " \[LongDash] provided ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c \[NotEqual] 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "But what about the point represented by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ u + b\ v\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "?  This is a point at the line at infinity, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z == PE[u, v]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Such a point can be determined by the following kind of intersection:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PE[PE[u, v], PE[w, a\ u\  + \ b\ v\  + w]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(-a\)\ u - b\ v\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "But we already know representatives of the images under the transformation \
",
  StyleBox["T", "InlineInput"],
  " of the the four points being used here, and so we can calculate by \
substitution again:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Collect[#, {a, b, c}]&\)/@
      \((Tidy/@Factor[
            Release[Hold[PE[PE[u, v], PE[w, a\ u\  + \ b\ v\  + w]]] /. \n
                \t\t\t\t{u \[Rule] \((a\_1\ u + b\_1\ v + c\_1\ w)\), \n
                  \t\t\t\t\tv \[Rule] \((a\_2\ u + b\_2\ v + c\_2\ w)\), \n
                  \t\t\t\t\tw \[Rule] \((a\_3\ u + b\_3\ v + c\_3\ w)\)}]])
        \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(-\((
          a\ \((u\ a\_1 + v\ b\_1 + w\ c\_1)\) + 
            b\ \((u\ a\_2 + v\ b\_2 + w\ c\_2)\))\)\)\ 
      \((\(-a\_3\)\ b\_2\ c\_1 + a\_2\ b\_3\ c\_1 + a\_3\ b\_1\ c\_2 - 
          a\_1\ b\_3\ c\_2 - a\_2\ b\_1\ c\_3 + a\_1\ b\_2\ c\_3)\)\)], 
  "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This shows that the point represented by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ u + b\ v\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " transforms under ",
  StyleBox["T", "InlineInput"],
  " to a point represented by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ A + b\ B\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  We have therefore proved that in general the point represented by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ u + b\ v + c\ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " transforms under ",
  StyleBox["T", "InlineInput"],
  " to a point represented by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ A + b\ B + c\ C\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  But this ",
  StyleBox["means exactly",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " that ",
  StyleBox["T", "InlineInput"],
  " is represented by a linear transformation.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Transformations and matrices", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  PageBreakAbove->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Point transformations by matrices", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If ",
  StyleBox["P", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ia a point, then, in the scheme we have been using, it is represented by \
a linear form, say, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P == a\ u + b\ v + c\ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  The ",
  StyleBox["point coordinates ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "of the point are of course ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({a, b, c}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  They can be calculated (in one way) by forming dot products with the \
the linear forms ",
  StyleBox["x", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["y", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["z", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  We call the procedure for doing this ",
  StyleBox["CP", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and it has this code in the ",
  StyleBox["Implementation.nb", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell["CP[p_] := Apply[PD,{{x,p},{y,p},{z,p}},{1}]; ", "Input",
  CellFrame->True,
  Evaluatable->False,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If, on the other hand, ",
  StyleBox["l", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is a line, then the ",
  StyleBox["line coordinates",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["CL[l]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", can be calculated with the dual dot products as follows:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["CL[l_] := Apply[PD,{{l,u},{l,v},{l,w}},{1}];", "Input",
  CellFrame->True,
  Evaluatable->False,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If we have a list of points (or lines) of length ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", then we can form an ",
  StyleBox["nx3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["matrix",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of coordinates by these procedures:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
MP[s_] := Map[CP,s];
ML[s_] := Map[CL,s];\
\>", "Input",
  CellFrame->True,
  Evaluatable->False,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell["\<\
Here are some examples of point and line coordinates using these \
functions:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[a, b, c]; \)\), 
    \(CP[3\ u - 4\ v + a\ w + b\ v]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({3, \(-4\) + b, a}\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(tt = Union@@Outer[PE, {u, u + v, u + 2\ v}, {v, v + w, v - w}]; 
    \)\), 
    \(TableForm[tt]\), 
    \(TableForm[ML[tt]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    InterpretationBox[GridBox[{
          {"z"},
          {\(\(-y\) + z\)},
          {\(x - y + z\)},
          {\(2\ x - y + z\)},
          {\(y + z\)},
          {\(\(-2\)\ x + y + z\)},
          {\(\(-x\) + y + z\)}
          },
        RowSpacings->1,
        ColumnSpacings->3,
        RowAlignments->Baseline,
        ColumnAlignments->{Left}],
      TableForm[ {z, 
        Plus[ 
          Times[ -1, y], z], 
        Plus[ x, 
          Times[ -1, y], z], 
        Plus[ 
          Times[ 2, x], 
          Times[ -1, y], z], 
        Plus[ y, z], 
        Plus[ 
          Times[ -2, x], y, z], 
        Plus[ 
          Times[ -1, x], y, z]}]]], "Output"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[GridBox[{
          {"0", "0", "1"},
          {"0", \(-1\), "1"},
          {"1", \(-1\), "1"},
          {"2", \(-1\), "1"},
          {"0", "1", "1"},
          {\(-2\), "1", "1"},
          {\(-1\), "1", "1"}
          },
        RowSpacings->1,
        ColumnSpacings->3,
        RowAlignments->Baseline,
        ColumnAlignments->{Left}],
      (TableForm[ #]&)]], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell["Here are some additional trials:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[a, b, c, Subscript]; 
    \n{P\_1, P\_2, P\_3} = {a\_1\ u + b\_1\ v + c\_1\ w, 
        a\_2\ u + b\_2\ v + c\_2\ w, a\_3\ u + b\_3\ v + c\_3\ w}; \)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(CP[P\_1]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a\_1, b\_1, c\_1}\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(MatrixForm[MP[{P\_1, P\_2, P\_3}]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[
      RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
            {\(a\_1\), \(b\_1\), \(c\_1\)},
            {\(a\_2\), \(b\_2\), \(c\_2\)},
            {\(a\_3\), \(b\_3\), \(c\_3\)}
            }], ")"}],
      (MatrixForm[ #]&)]], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(MatrixForm[ML[{PE[P\_2, P\_3], PE[P\_1, P\_3], PE[P\_1, P\_2]}]]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[
      RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
            {\(\(-b\_3\)\ c\_2 + b\_2\ c\_3\), \(a\_3\ c\_2 - a\_2\ c\_3\), 
              \(\(-a\_3\)\ b\_2 + a\_2\ b\_3\)},
            {\(\(-b\_3\)\ c\_1 + b\_1\ c\_3\), \(a\_3\ c\_1 - a\_1\ c\_3\), 
              \(\(-a\_3\)\ b\_1 + a\_1\ b\_3\)},
            {\(\(-b\_2\)\ c\_1 + b\_1\ c\_2\), \(a\_2\ c\_1 - a\_1\ c\_2\), 
              \(\(-a\_2\)\ b\_1 + a\_1\ b\_2\)}
            }], ")"}],
      (MatrixForm[ #]&)]], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, as we noted earlier, every ",
  StyleBox["non-singular",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["3x3",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " matrix ",
  StyleBox["M", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " determines a linear transformation of vectors, and hence determines ",
  StyleBox["projective point transformation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".   The question is how to use the matrix to calculate the transformation \
on linear forms."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If the vector ",
  StyleBox["V", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " gives the homogeneous coordinates of a ",
  StyleBox["point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", then \[LongDash] by the convention we normally use in assigning ",
  StyleBox["coordinates to a transformation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " \[LongDash] the matrix multiplication ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(V . M\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " will give the coordinates of the ",
  StyleBox["image point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".   ",
  "The reason for stressing non-singularity is that the transformation should \
be",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["everywhere defined",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Thus, if the vector ",
  StyleBox["V", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is non zero, then so should be ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(V . M\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Moreover, the transformation should be ",
  StyleBox["invertible",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "so that it is one-one.  The non-singular matrices are exaclty thouse that \
have inverses, and we know ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(V . M . Inverse[M] == V\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "With the primitive operations of linear algebra, however, a given matrix \
operates only on the ",
  StyleBox["vectors",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (coordinates).  We need to consider how this matrix also operates on ",
  StyleBox["linear forms",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (and, for later use, on ",
  StyleBox["polynomials",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in general)",
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  And, in choosing this set-up, we have taken points as primary; we could \
just as well as taken lines first.  The two points of view, however, are \
easily related by means of ",
  StyleBox["duality",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with an explicit definition of ",
  StyleBox["dual",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["matrix",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", as we will show."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The definition of the needed calculation is easy: the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P == a\ u + b\ v + c\ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is transformed to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(CP[P] . M . {u, v, w}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", because the second dot is required to go back ",
  StyleBox["from",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " coordinates ",
  StyleBox["to",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " a linear ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-form.  Let us take an example."
}], "Text"],

Cell["Consider the following matrix.  (Is it non-singular?)", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(M = {{1, 1, 1}, {1, 2, 3}, {1, 4, 9}}; \nM // MatrixForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[
      RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
            {"1", "1", "1"},
            {"1", "2", "3"},
            {"1", "4", "9"}
            }], ")"}],
      (MatrixForm[ #]&)]], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is the linear transformation given by ",
  StyleBox["M", "InlineInput"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    RowBox[{
      StyleBox[\(P = a\ u + b\ v + c\ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"], 
      StyleBox[";",
        FontWeight->"Bold"], "\n", 
      StyleBox[\(CP[P] . M . {u, v, w} // Tidy\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((a + b + c)\)\ u + \((a + 2\ b + 4\ c)\)\ v + 
      \((a + 3\ b + 9\ c)\)\ w\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "However, we may interpret the rows of this matrix as being the coordinates \
of the ",
  StyleBox["images",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the fundamental points ",
  StyleBox["u", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["v", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["w", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Their coordinates are the obvious ones: "
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["MP[{u,v,w}]//MatrixForm", "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[
      RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
            {"1", "0", "0"},
            {"0", "1", "0"},
            {"0", "0", "1"}
            }], ")"}],
      (MatrixForm[ #]&)]], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, by the convention adopted, the image of ",
  StyleBox["u", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "under the transformation given by ",
  StyleBox["M", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " has exactly  the coordinates ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({1, 0, 0} . M\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".   Thus, it should be clear that, when we form the matrix product:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["M.{u,v,w} ", "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({u + v + w, u + 2\ v + 3\ w, u + 4\ v + 9\ w}\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "we are calculating dot products that give us the forms representing the ",
  StyleBox["images",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  StyleBox["all three",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  of the basis forms at the same time. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Conversely, if we have a triple of points and want the matrix that gives a \
transformation taking ",
  StyleBox["{u,v,w}",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to the given triple, we can use this kind of computation to find it:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(MatrixForm[MP[{u + v + w, u + 2\ v + 3\ w, u + 4\ v + 9\ w}]]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[
      RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
            {"1", "1", "1"},
            {"1", "2", "3"},
            {"1", "4", "9"}
            }], ")"}],
      (MatrixForm[ #]&)]], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Warning.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " Many vectors represent the ",
  StyleBox["same",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " point, and in a similar way, many matrices represent the ",
  StyleBox["same",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " transformation.  In fact, since any ",
  StyleBox["non-zero multiple",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the vector ",
  StyleBox["V", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " represents the",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["same",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", any ",
  StyleBox["non-zero multiple",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the matrix ",
  StyleBox["M", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " represents the ",
  StyleBox["same",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["transformation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", because the operation ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(V . M\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is ",
  StyleBox["bilinear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ". "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Next, to show how a mapping transforms",
  StyleBox[" (homogeneous) polynomial forms of arbitrary degree",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", it is simpler to use a ",
  StyleBox["substitution",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " by substituting the new linear polynomials in for the fundamental ones.  \
We can program this transformation by simultaneous substitution very easily \
in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  as a function on any ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials by the following command:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData["TP[m_]:=Tidy[#/.Thread[{u,v,w}\[Rule]m.{u,v,w}]]&;"], "Input",
  CellFrame->True,
  Evaluatable->False,
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(TP[M]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is of course a ",
  StyleBox["linear transformation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of linear ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-forms.  (Why?)  We read ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(TP[M]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " as: ",
  StyleBox[
  "\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]the (projective) transformation with point matrix  \
",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["mm",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote].",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "   We note we will also need some additional notation for forming \
transformations in order to define them in a more ",
  StyleBox["geometric way",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This next example incidentially shows why it is convenient to define ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(TP[M]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "as having a ",
  StyleBox["function as a value",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(M = {{1, 1, 1}, {1, 2, 3}, {1, 4, 9}}; 
    TP[M]/@{u, v, w, a\ u + b\ v + c\ w, u\^2 + v\ w, 
          \((u - v + 2\ w)\)\^3} // TableForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    InterpretationBox[GridBox[{
          {\(u + v + w\)},
          {\(u + 2\ v + 3\ w\)},
          {\(u + 4\ v + 9\ w\)},
          {
            \(\((a + b + c)\)\ u + \((a + 2\ b + 4\ c)\)\ v + 
              \((a + 3\ b + 9\ c)\)\ w\)},
          {\(2\ u\^2 + 8\ u\ v + 9\ v\^2 + 14\ u\ w + 32\ v\ w + 28\ w\^2\)},
          {
            \(8\ u\^3 + 84\ u\^2\ v + 294\ u\ v\^2 + 343\ v\^3 + 
              192\ u\^2\ w + 1344\ u\ v\ w + 2352\ v\^2\ w + 1536\ u\ w\^2 + 
              5376\ v\ w\^2 + 4096\ w\^3\)}
          },
        RowSpacings->1,
        ColumnSpacings->3,
        RowAlignments->Baseline,
        ColumnAlignments->{Left}],
      TableForm[ {
        Plus[ u, v, w], 
        Plus[ u, 
          Times[ 2, v], 
          Times[ 3, w]], 
        Plus[ u, 
          Times[ 4, v], 
          Times[ 9, w]], 
        Plus[ 
          Times[ 
            Plus[ a, b, c], u], 
          Times[ 
            Plus[ a, 
              Times[ 2, b], 
              Times[ 4, c]], v], 
          Times[ 
            Plus[ a, 
              Times[ 3, b], 
              Times[ 9, c]], w]], 
        Plus[ 
          Times[ 2, 
            Power[ u, 2]], 
          Times[ 8, u, v], 
          Times[ 9, 
            Power[ v, 2]], 
          Times[ 14, u, w], 
          Times[ 32, v, w], 
          Times[ 28, 
            Power[ w, 2]]], 
        Plus[ 
          Times[ 8, 
            Power[ u, 3]], 
          Times[ 84, 
            Power[ u, 2], v], 
          Times[ 294, u, 
            Power[ v, 2]], 
          Times[ 343, 
            Power[ v, 3]], 
          Times[ 192, 
            Power[ u, 2], w], 
          Times[ 1344, u, v, w], 
          Times[ 2352, 
            Power[ v, 2], w], 
          Times[ 1536, u, 
            Power[ w, 2]], 
          Times[ 5376, v, 
            Power[ w, 2]], 
          Times[ 4096, 
            Power[ w, 3]]]}]]], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "To review what we have done, the relationship between point coordinates, \
matrix multiplication, and linear forms is fully established by the following \
symbolic equalities which  can be checked in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " :"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(CP[\(TP[M]\)[a\ u + b\ v + c\ w]] == {a, b, c} . M\), 
    \(\(TP[M]\)[a\ u + b\ v + c\ w] == {a, b, c} . M . {u, v, w}\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(True\)], "Output"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(True\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In other words, we have two ways of writing the same thing: in terms of ",
  StyleBox["linear forms",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and in terms of ",
  StyleBox["vectors of coordinates",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  But, there is also another point of view."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment 1.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Since, ",
  StyleBox["TP[M]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is defined on polynomials by ",
  StyleBox["substitution",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and since substitution preserves all ring operations on polynomials, \
this remark also proves that ",
  StyleBox["TP[M]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is indeed a ring automorphism \[LongDash] or better an ",
  StyleBox["algebra",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["automorphism",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", since ",
  StyleBox["TP[M]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " also preserves scalar multiplication."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment 2.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Conversely, ",
  StyleBox["every",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " algebra endomorphism obviously works by substitution; and if such a \
mapping did not map the generators ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({u, v, w}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to ",
  StyleBox["linear forms",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", it could not be surjective, since otherwise it would ",
  StyleBox["raise degrees",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Thus, ",
  StyleBox["all",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " algebra ",
  StyleBox["automorphisms",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " are of the form ",
  StyleBox["TP[M]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[".  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Compositions of transformations", "Subsubsection",
  PageBreakAbove->True],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Concerning the ",
  StyleBox["composition",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of transformations, the conventional definitions of matrix multiplication \
and the way we have utilized coordinates gives the following result:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Given matrices ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then the two functions \n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(TP[M\_1 . M\_2]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Composition[TP[M\_2], TP[M\_1]]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox["\n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "are the ",
  StyleBox["same function",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".   "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "(Note the ",
  StyleBox["order!",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ")  We will prove this by example, but it can also be proved in generality \
using symbolic matrices.  That is to say, in using matrix multiplication you \
have to read ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\_1 . M\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " as meaning ",
  StyleBox["first",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  do transformation ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["then",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " do ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".   In terms of ",
  StyleBox["coordinates",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", a vector ",
  StyleBox["V", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is mapped to the vector\n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\((V . M\_1)\) . M\_2 == V . \((M\_1 . M\_2)\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " \nunder the composite transformation."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Perhaps here it is good to recall how ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " treats the composition of functions:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Composition[F, G]\)[x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(F[G[x]]\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The following commands in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  should be self-explainatory."
}], "Text",
  PageBreakAbove->False],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(M\_1 = {{0, 1, 0}, {0, 1, 1}, {1, 1, 0}}; \)\), 
    \(\(M\_2 = {{1, 1, 1}, {1, 2, 3}, {1, 4, 9}}; \)\)}], "Input",
  PageBreakAbove->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(M\_1 . {u, v, w}\), 
    \(TP[M\_1]/@{u, v, w}\)}], "Input",
  PageBreakAbove->False],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({v, v + w, u + v}\)], "Output"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({v, v + w, u + v}\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(TP[M\_2]/@{v, v + w, u + v}\), 
    \(MatrixForm[M\_1 . M\_2]\)}], "Input",
  PageBreakAbove->False],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({u + 2\ v + 3\ w, 2\ u + 6\ v + 12\ w, 2\ u + 3\ v + 4\ w}\)], "Output"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[
      RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
            {"1", "2", "3"},
            {"2", "6", "12"},
            {"2", "3", "4"}
            }], ")"}],
      (MatrixForm[ #]&)]], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(MatrixForm[M\_2 . M\_1]\)], "Input",
  PageBreakAbove->False],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[
      RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
            {"1", "3", "1"},
            {"3", "6", "2"},
            {"9", "14", "4"}
            }], ")"}],
      (MatrixForm[ #]&)]], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(M\_1 . M\_2 . {u, v, w}\), 
    \(TP[M\_1 . M\_2]/@{u, v, w}\), 
    \(Composition[TP[M\_2], TP[M\_1]]/@{u, v, w}\)}], "Input",
  PageBreakAbove->False],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({u + 2\ v + 3\ w, 2\ u + 6\ v + 12\ w, 2\ u + 3\ v + 4\ w}\)], "Output"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({u + 2\ v + 3\ w, 2\ u + 6\ v + 12\ w, 2\ u + 3\ v + 4\ w}\)], "Output"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({u + 2\ v + 3\ w, 2\ u + 6\ v + 12\ w, 2\ u + 3\ v + 4\ w}\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Corollary.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "In view of the law of composition, the two transformations ",
  StyleBox["TP[M]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["TP[Inverse[M]]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "must be ",
  StyleBox["inverses of each other",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " \[LongDash] assuming, of course, that the matrix ",
  StyleBox["M", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["non singular",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Line transformations by matrices", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Given the matrix ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " of a ",
  StyleBox["point transformation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", the next problem is to define the corresponding representation of the ",
  StyleBox["line transformation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (or, more generally, the action of the transformation on the dual ring of \
",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials).  That is, we have already defined ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(TP[M\_1]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " as an automorphism of the algebra of ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials, and so, we should make ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(TP[M\_1]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " an automorphism of the algebra of ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials.  The question is: ",
  StyleBox["which automorphism",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "?  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The algebraic answer is that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(TL[M\_1]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " has to be defined sot that it acts dually to preserve",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["the dot product",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " between linear forms:  \n",
  StyleBox["\t\t",
    Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[\(TL[M\_1]\)[l], \(TP[M\_1]\)[P]] == \(PD[l, P] . \)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]],
    Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],
  "\nWe call this the ",
  StyleBox["Transformation Law",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Rewriting the above to use coordinates on both sides, this requirement \
means that we are looking for a matrix ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "to operate on the line coordinates (in a dual way) so that: \n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(CP[P] . M\_1 . M\_2 . CL[l] == CP[P] . CL[l]\)], "Input",
    CellFrame->False,
    Evaluatable->False,
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    Background->None]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Since the point and line coordinates are ",
  StyleBox["arbitrary",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", the universal validity of this equation means that the product ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(M\_1 . M\_2\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " must be ",
  StyleBox["the identity matrix",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  The answer we are looking for is that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\_2 == Inverse[M\_1]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Thus, the implementation we provide reads:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(TL[m_] := 
      Tidy[#1 /. Thread[{x, y, z} \[Rule] {x, y, z} . Inverse[m]]]&\)], 
  "Input",
  CellFrame->True,
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The implementation makes the Transformation Law hold ",
  StyleBox["by definition",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  We now have to do some examples to check that this makes sense.  We \
again take the matrix we used earlier, but the reader can try another matrix \
on the same polynomials."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(M = {{1, 1, 1}, {1, 2, 3}, {1, 4, 9}}; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["First think of a line and a point:", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[3\ x + 4\ y - 7\ z, u - 7\ v - w]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(-18\)\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The point is ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the line.  Here are the transforms:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(TL[M]\)[3\ x + 4\ y - 7\ z]\), 
    \(\(TP[M]\)[u - 7\ v - w]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(-\(\(9\ x\)\/2\)\) + 14\ y - \(13\ z\)\/2\)], "Output"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(-7\)\ u - 17\ v - 29\ w\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And here is the ",
  StyleBox["PD",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " computation:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[\(TL[M]\)[3\ x + 4\ y - 7\ z], \(TP[M]\)[u - 7\ v - w]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(-18\)\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The transformed point is also ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the transformed line.  And here is a symbolic computation:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[a, b, c, Subscript]; \)\), 
    \(l = a\_1\ x + a\_2\ y + a\_3\ z; \nP = b\_1\ u + b\_2\ v + b\_3\ w; \n\n
    PD[l, P]\), 
    \(PD[\(TL[M]\)[l], \(TP[M]\)[P]]\ \), 
    \(PD[\(TL[M]\)[l], \(TP[M]\)[P]]\  // Expand\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a\_1\ a\_2 + b\_1\ b\_2 + c\_1\ c\_2\)], "Output"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((3\ a\_1 - \(5\ b\_1\)\/2 + c\_1\/2)\)\ \((a\_2 + b\_2 + c\_2)\) + 
      \((\(-3\)\ a\_1 + 4\ b\_1 - c\_1)\)\ \((a\_2 + 2\ b\_2 + 4\ c\_2)\) + 
      \((a\_1 - \(3\ b\_1\)\/2 + c\_1\/2)\)\ 
        \((a\_2 + 3\ b\_2 + 9\ c\_2)\)\)], "Output"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a\_1\ a\_2 + b\_1\ b\_2 + c\_1\ c\_2\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The intermediate result looked bad, but for this particular matrix ",
  StyleBox["M", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " the dot product is indeed preserved as desired.  Therefore, if ",
  StyleBox["P", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is a point on a line ",
  StyleBox["l", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", then ",
  StyleBox["TP[M][P]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is a point on the line ",
  StyleBox["TL[M][l]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  And the converse holds.  Moreover, ",
  StyleBox["any",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " point on ",
  StyleBox["TL[M][l]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "can be found this way.  (Why?)  In general, we set things up so that \
because projective transformations (as we have represented and defined them \
using ",
  StyleBox["matrices",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ") preserve ",
  StyleBox["PD", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", they preserve",
  StyleBox[" incidence of points and lines",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  This gives us the ",
  StyleBox["geometric",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " significance of the computations with matrices.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Note also the following immediate consequence of the transformation \
law by making a substitution and using the composition law:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Corollary.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["If ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["M", "InlineInput"],
  StyleBox[" is a non-singular matrix, and if ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["P",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" and ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "is a",
  StyleBox[" linear ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["-forms, and ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["l", "InlineInput"],
  StyleBox[" is a linear ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-form, then",
  StyleBox[":",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["\n\t",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(PD[\(TL[Inverse[M]]\)[l], P] == \(PD[l, \(TP[M]\)[P]] . \)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In the language of linear algebra, this means that the line transformation \
",
  StyleBox["TL[Inverse[M]]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is the ",
  StyleBox["adjoint",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the point transformation ",
  StyleBox["TP[M]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  This is to be interpreted in the sense of linear-space theory, where \
the two spaces of linear homogeneous polynomials are made ",
  StyleBox["dual spaces",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of one another under the pairing ",
  StyleBox["PD[l, P]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
We next want to do some calculations of meets and joins of lines \
and points to see how well the matrix representation works out.  We again \
take the matrix we used earlier.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(M = {{1, 1, 1}, {1, 2, 3}, {1, 4, 9}}; \nP\_1 = u - w; \n
    P\_2 = u + v + 2\ w; \nl = PE[P\_1, P\_2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(x - 3\ y + z\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell["A line has joined two points.  Let us transform it.", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(TL[M]\)[l]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(11\ x - 16\ y + 6\ z\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell["What about the line joining the transform of the two points?", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PE[\(TP[M]\)[P\_1], \(TP[M]\)[P\_2]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(22\ x - 32\ y + 12\ z\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We see that the two polynomials are ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "the same, but they represent the same line \[LongDash] after multiplying \
one of them by the scalar 2.  (Try it with another matrix!)  This holds \
generally.   Geometrically this means that the image of the line joing two \
points is the line joining the image of the two points.  In other words, ",
  StyleBox["projective transformations preserve joins (and meets)",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", as is intended.  What we are checking here is that the computations do \
indeed work out in special cases."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here are some other calculations with more general points and lines.  We \
note that the scalar factor that comes up in comapring linear forms is the ",
  StyleBox["determinant",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the matrix."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Det[M]", "Input",
  PageBreakAbove->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(2\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(P\_1 = a\_1\ u + a\_2\ v + a\_3\ w; \n
    P\_2 = b\_1\ u + b\_2\ v + b\_3\ w; \n\(TL[M]\)[PE[P\_1, P\_2]]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(y\ \((
          a\_2\ b\_1 + 4\ a\_3\ b\_1 - a\_1\ b\_2 + 3\ a\_3\ b\_2 - 
            4\ a\_1\ b\_3 - 3\ a\_2\ b\_3)\) + 
      z\ \((\(-\(1\/2\)\)\ a\_2\ b\_1 - \(3\ a\_3\ b\_1\)\/2 + 
            \(a\_1\ b\_2\)\/2 - a\_3\ b\_2 + \(3\ a\_1\ b\_3\)\/2 + 
            a\_2\ b\_3)\) + 
      x\ \((\(-\(1\/2\)\)\ a\_2\ b\_1 - \(5\ a\_3\ b\_1\)\/2 + 
            \(a\_1\ b\_2\)\/2 - 3\ a\_3\ b\_2 + \(5\ a\_1\ b\_3\)\/2 + 
            3\ a\_2\ b\_3)\)\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PE[\(TP[M]\)[P\_1], \(TP[M]\)[P\_2]] // Tidy\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(y\ \((
          2\ a\_2\ b\_1 + 8\ a\_3\ b\_1 - 2\ a\_1\ b\_2 + 6\ a\_3\ b\_2 - 
            8\ a\_1\ b\_3 - 6\ a\_2\ b\_3)\) + 
      z\ \((\(-a\_2\)\ b\_1 - 3\ a\_3\ b\_1 + a\_1\ b\_2 - 2\ a\_3\ b\_2 + 
            3\ a\_1\ b\_3 + 2\ a\_2\ b\_3)\) + 
      x\ \((\(-a\_2\)\ b\_1 - 5\ a\_3\ b\_1 + a\_1\ b\_2 - 6\ a\_3\ b\_2 + 
            5\ a\_1\ b\_3 + 6\ a\_2\ b\_3)\)\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell["Here is a dual calculation:", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(l\_1 = c\_1\ x + c\_2\ y + c\_3\ z; \n
    l\_2 = d\_1\ x + d\_2\ y + d\_3\ z; \n\(TP[M]\)[PE[l\_1, l\_2]]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(w\ \((
          \(-9\)\ c\_2\ d\_1 + 3\ c\_3\ d\_1 + 9\ c\_1\ d\_2 - c\_3\ d\_2 - 
            3\ c\_1\ d\_3 + c\_2\ d\_3)\) + 
      v\ \((\(-4\)\ c\_2\ d\_1 + 2\ c\_3\ d\_1 + 4\ c\_1\ d\_2 - c\_3\ d\_2 - 
            2\ c\_1\ d\_3 + c\_2\ d\_3)\) + 
      u\ \((\(-c\_2\)\ d\_1 + c\_3\ d\_1 + c\_1\ d\_2 - c\_3\ d\_2 - 
            c\_1\ d\_3 + c\_2\ d\_3)\)\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PE[\(TL[M]\)[l\_1], \(TL[M]\)[l\_2]] // Tidy\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(w\ \((
          \(-\(9\/2\)\)\ c\_2\ d\_1 + \(3\ c\_3\ d\_1\)\/2 + 
            \(9\ c\_1\ d\_2\)\/2 - \(c\_3\ d\_2\)\/2 - \(3\ c\_1\ d\_3\)\/2 + 
            \(c\_2\ d\_3\)\/2)\) + 
      v\ \((\(-2\)\ c\_2\ d\_1 + c\_3\ d\_1 + 2\ c\_1\ d\_2 - 
            \(c\_3\ d\_2\)\/2 - c\_1\ d\_3 + \(c\_2\ d\_3\)\/2)\) + 
      u\ \((\(-\(1\/2\)\)\ c\_2\ d\_1 + \(c\_3\ d\_1\)\/2 + 
            \(c\_1\ d\_2\)\/2 - \(c\_3\ d\_2\)\/2 - \(c\_1\ d\_3\)\/2 + 
            \(c\_2\ d\_3\)\/2)\)\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Or we can try a completely general ",
  StyleBox["symbolic matrix",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and verify the law that says that after transformation, exterior products \
are multiplied by the determinant of the matrix."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(M = {{r\_1, r\_2, r\_3}, {s\_1, s\_2, s\_3}, {t\_1, t\_2, t\_3}}; \n
    MatrixForm[M]\), 
    \(det = Det[M]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[
      RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
            {\(r\_1\), \(r\_2\), \(r\_3\)},
            {\(s\_1\), \(s\_2\), \(s\_3\)},
            {\(t\_1\), \(t\_2\), \(t\_3\)}
            }], ")"}],
      (MatrixForm[ #]&)]], "Output"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(-r\_3\)\ s\_2\ t\_1 + r\_2\ s\_3\ t\_1 + r\_3\ s\_1\ t\_2 - 
      r\_1\ s\_3\ t\_2 - r\_2\ s\_1\ t\_3 + r\_1\ s\_2\ t\_3\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now here is a calculation that takes ",
  StyleBox["quite a lot",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of algebra (and ",
  StyleBox["Simplify", "InlineInput"],
  " had to be put to work to cancel fractions) :"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(P\_1 = a\_1\ u + a\_2\ v + a\_3\ w; \n
    P\_2 = b\_1\ u + b\_2\ v + b\_3\ w; \n\n
    \(det\ \(TL[M]\)[PE[P\_1, P\_2]]\  // Simplify\) // Tidy\n\), 
    \(\(PE[\(TP[M]\)[P\_1], \(TP[M]\)[P\_2]] // Expand\) // Tidy\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(z\ \((
          a\_2\ b\_1\ r\_2\ s\_1 - a\_1\ b\_2\ r\_2\ s\_1 - 
            a\_2\ b\_1\ r\_1\ s\_2 + a\_1\ b\_2\ r\_1\ s\_2 + 
            a\_3\ b\_1\ r\_2\ t\_1 - a\_1\ b\_3\ r\_2\ t\_1 + 
            a\_3\ b\_2\ s\_2\ t\_1 - a\_2\ b\_3\ s\_2\ t\_1 - 
            a\_3\ b\_1\ r\_1\ t\_2 + a\_1\ b\_3\ r\_1\ t\_2 - 
            a\_3\ b\_2\ s\_1\ t\_2 + a\_2\ b\_3\ s\_1\ t\_2)\) + 
      y\ \((\(-a\_2\)\ b\_1\ r\_3\ s\_1 + a\_1\ b\_2\ r\_3\ s\_1 + 
            a\_2\ b\_1\ r\_1\ s\_3 - a\_1\ b\_2\ r\_1\ s\_3 - 
            a\_3\ b\_1\ r\_3\ t\_1 + a\_1\ b\_3\ r\_3\ t\_1 - 
            a\_3\ b\_2\ s\_3\ t\_1 + a\_2\ b\_3\ s\_3\ t\_1 + 
            a\_3\ b\_1\ r\_1\ t\_3 - a\_1\ b\_3\ r\_1\ t\_3 + 
            a\_3\ b\_2\ s\_1\ t\_3 - a\_2\ b\_3\ s\_1\ t\_3)\) + 
      x\ \((a\_2\ b\_1\ r\_3\ s\_2 - a\_1\ b\_2\ r\_3\ s\_2 - 
            a\_2\ b\_1\ r\_2\ s\_3 + a\_1\ b\_2\ r\_2\ s\_3 + 
            a\_3\ b\_1\ r\_3\ t\_2 - a\_1\ b\_3\ r\_3\ t\_2 + 
            a\_3\ b\_2\ s\_3\ t\_2 - a\_2\ b\_3\ s\_3\ t\_2 - 
            a\_3\ b\_1\ r\_2\ t\_3 + a\_1\ b\_3\ r\_2\ t\_3 - 
            a\_3\ b\_2\ s\_2\ t\_3 + a\_2\ b\_3\ s\_2\ t\_3)\)\)], "Output"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(z\ \((
          a\_2\ b\_1\ r\_2\ s\_1 - a\_1\ b\_2\ r\_2\ s\_1 - 
            a\_2\ b\_1\ r\_1\ s\_2 + a\_1\ b\_2\ r\_1\ s\_2 + 
            a\_3\ b\_1\ r\_2\ t\_1 - a\_1\ b\_3\ r\_2\ t\_1 + 
            a\_3\ b\_2\ s\_2\ t\_1 - a\_2\ b\_3\ s\_2\ t\_1 - 
            a\_3\ b\_1\ r\_1\ t\_2 + a\_1\ b\_3\ r\_1\ t\_2 - 
            a\_3\ b\_2\ s\_1\ t\_2 + a\_2\ b\_3\ s\_1\ t\_2)\) + 
      y\ \((\(-a\_2\)\ b\_1\ r\_3\ s\_1 + a\_1\ b\_2\ r\_3\ s\_1 + 
            a\_2\ b\_1\ r\_1\ s\_3 - a\_1\ b\_2\ r\_1\ s\_3 - 
            a\_3\ b\_1\ r\_3\ t\_1 + a\_1\ b\_3\ r\_3\ t\_1 - 
            a\_3\ b\_2\ s\_3\ t\_1 + a\_2\ b\_3\ s\_3\ t\_1 + 
            a\_3\ b\_1\ r\_1\ t\_3 - a\_1\ b\_3\ r\_1\ t\_3 + 
            a\_3\ b\_2\ s\_1\ t\_3 - a\_2\ b\_3\ s\_1\ t\_3)\) + 
      x\ \((a\_2\ b\_1\ r\_3\ s\_2 - a\_1\ b\_2\ r\_3\ s\_2 - 
            a\_2\ b\_1\ r\_2\ s\_3 + a\_1\ b\_2\ r\_2\ s\_3 + 
            a\_3\ b\_1\ r\_3\ t\_2 - a\_1\ b\_3\ r\_3\ t\_2 + 
            a\_3\ b\_2\ s\_3\ t\_2 - a\_2\ b\_3\ s\_3\ t\_2 - 
            a\_3\ b\_1\ r\_2\ t\_3 + a\_1\ b\_3\ r\_2\ t\_3 - 
            a\_3\ b\_2\ s\_2\ t\_3 + a\_2\ b\_3\ s\_2\ t\_3)\)\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell["\<\
This has been a proof of the following theorem about linear \
forms:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Theorem.",
  StyleBox["  If ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["M", "InlineInput"],
  StyleBox[" is a non-singular matrix, and if ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(P\_1\)]],
  StyleBox[" and ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(P\_2\)]],
  StyleBox[" are linear ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "uvw",
  StyleBox["-forms, then:",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PE[\(TP[M]\)[P\_1], \(TP[M]\)[P\_2]] == 
        Det[M]\ \(TL[M]\)[PE[P\_1, P\_2]]\)]]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And of course there is a dual theorem for ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-forms.  In fact, in some presentations of Linear Algebra, an equation \
like the above is used to ",
  StyleBox["define",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the determinant of a matrix  We had other definitions to use, and so we ",
  
  StyleBox["verified",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " this law.  Geometrically, it says that the line joining the transforms of \
two points ",
  StyleBox["is",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the transform of the line joining the original points.  We have perhaps \
not learned much new out of this, but we have seen that the algebra is ",
  StyleBox["coherent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Algebraically, linear substitution is an automorphism of the algebra \
structure of the polynomial ring, and this holds for the ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials as well as for the ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-polynomials.  So far we have been thinking in terms ",
  StyleBox["starting with",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the points rather than the lines.  In other words, we have to start with \
",
  StyleBox["M", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " coming about this way:\n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M == MP[TP[M]/@{u, v, w}]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "and then we know how to go from the transform ",
  StyleBox["TP[M]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to the appropriate transformation on lines, ",
  StyleBox["TL[M]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  The question is how to go ",
  StyleBox["the other way",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "To understand this, let us see what a typical ",
  StyleBox["M", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " does to the ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-basis. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(M = {{1, 1, 1}, {1, 2, 3}, {1, 4, 9}}; \)\), 
    \(TL[M]/@{x, y, z}\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({3\ x - 3\ y + z, \(-\(\(5\ x\)\/2\)\) + 4\ y - \(3\ z\)\/2, 
      x\/2 - y + z\/2}\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell["\<\
In other words, suppose we see in front of us the images of the \
three basic lines.  These images give us the following matrix:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(M\_0 = ML[%]; \nM\_0 // MatrixForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[
      RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
            {"3", \(-3\), "1"},
            {\(-\(5\/2\)\), "4", \(-\(3\/2\)\)},
            {\(1\/2\), \(-1\), \(1\/2\)}
            }], ")"}],
      (MatrixForm[ #]&)]], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "But, if we are thinking of ",
  StyleBox["point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "transformations, then we should treat line coordinates as ",
  StyleBox["dual",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " vectors to point coordinates.  This means that we really should take the \
",
  StyleBox["transpose",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the matrix ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\_0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Now, if we further take the ",
  StyleBox["inverse",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", then we will get back to the standard matrix of  the required ",
  StyleBox["point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " transformation."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Inverse[Transpose[M\_0]] // MatrixForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[
      RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
            {"1", "1", "1"},
            {"1", "2", "3"},
            {"1", "4", "9"}
            }], ")"}],
      (MatrixForm[ #]&)]], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In other words, if you ",
  StyleBox["know",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " where the lines ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["z",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " are going, then make a ",
  StyleBox["matrix",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\_0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " out of their coordinates.  The line transformation can then be calculated \
as ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(TL[Inverse[Transpose[M\_0]]]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Transforming quadrangles and quadrilaterals", "Section",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Mapping quadrangles", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Moving points", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["Consider the matrices:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(M\_1 = {{1, 1, 1}, {1, 2, 3}, {1, 4, 9}}; \n
    M\_2 = {{a, 0, 0}, {0, b, 0}, {0, 0, c}}; \)], "Input"],

Cell["Now calculate:", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(M\_2 . M\_1 // MatrixForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[
      RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
            {"a", "a", "a"},
            {"b", \(2\ b\), \(3\ b\)},
            {"c", \(4\ c\), \(9\ c\)}
            }], ")"}],
      (MatrixForm[ #]&)]], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The matrices ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\_2 . M\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are quite different, though related.  Clearly the second is formed from \
the first by multiplying the rows each by a different scalar.  Let us see \
what the matrices do to a certain set of points:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    RowBox[{"TableForm", "[", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{
            RowBox[{"TP", "[", 
              StyleBox[\(M\_1\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "]"}], "/@", \({u, v, w}\)}], ",", 
          RowBox[{
            RowBox[{"TP", "[", 
              StyleBox[\(M\_2 . M\_1\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "]"}], "/@", \({u, v, w}\)}]}], "}"}], 
      "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[GridBox[{
          {\(u + v + w\), \(u + 2\ v + 3\ w\), \(u + 4\ v + 9\ w\)},
          {\(a\ u + a\ v + a\ w\), \(b\ u + 2\ b\ v + 3\ b\ w\), 
            \(c\ u + 4\ c\ v + 9\ c\ w\)}
          },
        RowSpacings->1,
        ColumnSpacings->3,
        RowAlignments->Baseline,
        ColumnAlignments->{Left}],
      (TableForm[ #]&)]], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The linear forms ",
  StyleBox["u",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["v",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " are mapped to ",
  StyleBox["different",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " linear forms under the two matrices, but the results are equivalent \
representations of the",
  StyleBox[" same  three image points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".   So, as projective transformations, the point mappings associated with \
",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\_2 . M\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are equal on ",
  StyleBox["one triple of points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  But, as we have to stress over and over, this does not mean that they \
are the ",
  StyleBox["same",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " projective transformation.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let us see, indeed, what happens to another point, say, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u + v + w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{\(\(TP[M\_1]\)[u + v + w]\), 
    RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"TP", "[", 
          StyleBox[\(M\_2 . M\_1\),
            FontWeight->"Bold"], "]"}], "[", \(u + v + w\), "]"}], "//", 
      "Tidy"}]}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(3\ u + 7\ v + 13\ w\)], "Output"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((a + b + c)\)\ u + \((a + 2\ b + 4\ c)\)\ v + 
      \((a + 3\ b + 9\ c)\)\ w\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell["This can also be calculated as:", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a, b, c} . M\_1 . {u, v, w}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((a + b + c)\)\ u + \((a + 2\ b + 4\ c)\)\ v + 
      \((a + 3\ b + 9\ c)\)\ w\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The matrix ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " maps the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u + v + w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to a certain place, but ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\_2 . M\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " puts it in ",
  StyleBox["quite another",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " place.  In fact, we see from the third matrix form of the expression \
that, since ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is non singular, then ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({a, b, c} . M\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " can equal ",
  StyleBox["any  vector",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " we want.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Thus, by chossing these multipliers, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\_2 . M\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["free to move the point represented by the form ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u + v + w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  ",
  StyleBox["anywhere we desire.",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  One conclusion is that, therefore, three points and their images ",
  StyleBox["do not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " determine a projective transformation of the plane."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The projective transformation ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox["TP",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["[",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[
          TagBox[
            RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
                  {"a", "0", "0"},
                  {"0", "b", "0"},
                  {"0", "0", "c"}
                  }], ")"}],
            (MatrixForm[ #]&)],
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["]",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  " keeps the points represented by ",
  StyleBox["u", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["v", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["w", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " fixed, but it moves the point corresponding to the linear form ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u + v + w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to ",
  StyleBox["any other position",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", namely ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ u + b\ v + c\ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " (",
  StyleBox["via",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "the choice of the three multipliers ",
  StyleBox["a", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["b", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["c", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ") ."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Moving quadrangles", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["This theorem can then be improved to:", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Corollary 1.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Given four points, ",
  StyleBox["no three of which are collinear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", represented by linear forms  ",
  StyleBox["P", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["Q", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["R", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["S", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "then there is a matrix ",
  StyleBox["M", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " so that the projective transformation ",
  StyleBox["TP[M]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " moves the quadruple of points represented by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({u, v, w, u + v + w}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to the quadruple represented by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({P, Q, R, S}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Proof.",
  StyleBox["  First we note that ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["P", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["Q", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["and",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["R", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" are ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["linearly independent",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[".  So there is a matrix ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(M\_1\)]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["so that we have \n\t",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\(TP[M\_1]\)[u] == P\)]],
  ",\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\(TP[M\_1]\)[v] == Q\)]],
  ",\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\(TP[M\_1]\)[w] == R\)]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "But we would find that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[\(S\_1\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox["TP",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox["[",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              SubscriptBox[
                StyleBox["M",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"], 
              StyleBox["]",
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "[", \(u + v + w\), "]"}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  " does ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " represent the desired point.  Let instead ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[\(S\_2\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox["TP",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox["[",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              RowBox[{
                StyleBox["Inverse",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                StyleBox["[",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                SubscriptBox[
                  StyleBox["M",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"], 
                StyleBox["]",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
              StyleBox["]",
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "[", "S", "]"}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  ".  Now choose ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " so that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TP[M\_2]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "maps ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({u, v, w, u + v + w}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({a\ u, b\ v, c\ w, S\_2}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", for a suitable choice of scalars",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({a, b, c}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  We then let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\_3 == M\_2 . M\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  This matrix then satisfies the theorem, because ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TP[M\_3]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "maps ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({u, v, w, u + v + w}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({a\ P, b\ Q, c\ R, S}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Corollary 2.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Given four points, no three of which are collinear, represented by \
linear forms  ",
  StyleBox["P", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["Q", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["R", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["S", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and another such quadruple ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        SuperscriptBox["P", "\[Prime]",
          MultilineFunction->None],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        SuperscriptBox["Q", "\[Prime]",
          MultilineFunction->None],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        SuperscriptBox["R", "\[Prime]",
          MultilineFunction->None],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        SuperscriptBox["S", "\[Prime]",
          MultilineFunction->None],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then there is a matrix ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["M",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " so that the projective transformation ",
  StyleBox["TP[M]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " moves the quadruple of points represented by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({P, Q, R, S}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to the quadruple of points represented by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"{", 
          RowBox[{
            SuperscriptBox["P", "\[Prime]",
              MultilineFunction->None], ",", 
            SuperscriptBox["Q", "\[Prime]",
              MultilineFunction->None], ",", 
            SuperscriptBox["R", "\[Prime]",
              MultilineFunction->None], ",", 
            SuperscriptBox["S", "\[Prime]",
              MultilineFunction->None]}], "}"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Proof.",
  StyleBox[
  "  Move back and forth to the points represented by the standard quadruple \
",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \({u, v, w, u + v + w}\)]],
  StyleBox[", and then ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["compose",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" the transformations.  ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "Q.E.D."
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Suppose we have two, non-degenerate quadrangles.  ",
  StyleBox[
  "Can there be inequivalent matrices defining maps that take one to the \
other?",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "   "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Theorem.",
  StyleBox["  A projective transformation of the form ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["TP[mm]", "InlineInput"],
  StyleBox[" is ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["uniquely determined",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[
  " by four non-collinear points and their images, and two matrices determine \
the same projective transformation if, and only if, one is a scalar multiple \
of the other.",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Since any quadrangle can be mapped to or from the standard quadrangle ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({u, v, w, u + v + w}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", this is the same as asking whether there are matrices not equivalent to \
the identity matrix that map this  particular figure ",
  StyleBox["onto itself",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  (Permuting vertices is not allowed:  ",
  StyleBox["u",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " has to go to something equivalent, and the same must be true of the other \
points.)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Suppose the matrix ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["M",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "representing the transformation carrries ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({u, v, w}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({a\ u, b\ v, c\ w}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Then the matrix has to be",
  StyleBox[" \n\t\t\t",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"M", "==", 
          TagBox[
            RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
                  {"a", "0", "0"},
                  {"0", "b", "0"},
                  {"0", "0", "c"}
                  }], ")"}],
            (MatrixForm[ #]&)]}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  \nBut the transformation represented by this matrix carries the form ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u + v + w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ u + b\ v + c\ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  For these points to be equivalent, the multipliers ",
  StyleBox["a", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["b", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["c", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " all have to be ",
  StyleBox["equal",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Therefore, the matix is indeed equivalent to the identity matrix.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "To make ",
  StyleBox["calculations",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", we needed to implement these matrix manipulations.  THis has been done \
in ",
  StyleBox["Implementations.nb", "InlineInput"],
  ".  Supposing we are given the following two quadrangles:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Q = {u + v, v + w, w + u, u + v + w}; \n
    R = {u - 2\ v, v - 2\ w, w - 2\ u, u + 2\ v + 3\ w}; \)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Using the idea of keeping three points fixed and moving the fourth, \
we can implement the finding of the matrix of the transformation that will \
map the first given quadrangle to the second.  \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The operation is called ",
  StyleBox["MQP", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", standing for ",
  StyleBox[
  "\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]matrix for quadrangles of points\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote].  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "We test this out here.  (And the reader can easily change the cooeficients \
in the linear forms to give other tests, as well as look at the code.)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(M = MQP[Q, R]; \nM // MatrixForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[
      RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
            {"1", \(46\/7\), \(-\(43\/7\)\)},
            {\(-\(37\/7\)\), "2", \(43\/7\)},
            {\(37\/7\), \(-\(46\/7\)\), "3"}
            }], ")"}],
      (MatrixForm[ #]&)]], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Factor /@ (TP[M]/@{u+v, v+w, w+u, u+v+w})", "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({\(-\(30\/7\)\)\ \((u - 2\ v)\), \(-\(32\/7\)\)\ \((v - 2\ w)\), 
      22\/7\ \((2\ u - w)\), u + 2\ v + 3\ w}\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Except for some fractional multiples, the first quadrangle ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["Q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " as been moved to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["R",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Mapping quadrilaterals", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Doing calculations", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Using the same kind of martix manipulations as with quadrangles given by \
points, we have implemented the dual procedure of finding of the \
transformation that will map one given ",
  StyleBox["quadrilateral",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to another.  (Of course, we had to translate back from the dual matrix in \
order to put things in a standard form.)  Here is an example"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(K = {x + y, y - z, z + 2\ x, 3\ x + 4\ y + 5\ z}; \n
    L = {2\ x - y, 2\ y - z, 2\ z - x, x - y - z}; \nM = MQL[K, L]; \n
    M // MatrixForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[
      RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
            {\(312\/5\), \(99\/5\), \(198\/5\)},
            {\(202\/3\), \(89\/3\), \(101\/3\)},
            {\(-\(92\/15\)\), \(-\(184\/15\)\), \(17\/15\)}
            }], ")"}],
      (MatrixForm[ #]&)]], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor/@\(TL[M]/@K\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({1\/105\ \((2\ x - y)\), 3\/77\ \((2\ y - z)\), 
      \(-\(5\/42\)\)\ \((x - 2\ z)\), x - y - z}\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This is seen to be a list of lines equivalent line by line to those in ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["L",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  This means that ",
  StyleBox["TL[M]", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is doing the right thing.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We now going to put all of this to the ",
  StyleBox["acid test",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  " by ",
  StyleBox["converting the quadrilaterals into quadrangles",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", doing the calculations the first way, and then seeing if the results are \
the same.  The reasoning here is that mapping the given edges of a \
quadrilateral (regarded for the moment as a 4-gon) is the same as mapping the \
four vertices.  Well, we shall see!"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By the way, the operation ",
  StyleBox["PES", "InlineInput"],
  " applies the ",
  StyleBox["PE", "InlineInput"],
  " or join operation to the sequence of points in ",
  StyleBox["circular order",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", thus finding the ",
  StyleBox["sides",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of a polygon.  This is how we pass from the given points to the lines \
they determine."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(V = PES[K]\), 
    \(W = PES[L]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({\(-u\) + v + w, u - 2\ v - 2\ w, \(-4\)\ u - 7\ v + 8\ w, 
      \(-5\)\ u + 5\ v - w}\)], "Output"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({u + 2\ v + 4\ w, 4\ u + v + 2\ w, 2\ u + v + w, \(-u\) - 2\ v + w}\)], 
  "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor/@\(TP[M]/@V\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({\(-\(6\/5\)\)\ \((u + 2\ v + 4\ w)\), \(-15\)\ \((4\ u + v + 2\ w)\), 
      \(-385\)\ \((2\ u + v + w)\), 154\/5\ \((u + 2\ v - w)\)}\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So far so good: at least ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["M",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is doing its job.   What we do next is try to prove that if we compute \
the quadranglematrix by using the vertices, then we get a scalar multiple of \
",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["M",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Inverse[M].MQP[V,W]//MatrixForm", "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[
      RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
            {\(-\(5\/154\)\), "0", "0"},
            {"0", \(-\(5\/154\)\), "0"},
            {"0", "0", \(-\(5\/154\)\)}
            }], ")"}],
      (MatrixForm[ #]&)]], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["And it is a win!",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"]], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Three points and a line", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["But here is a different problem.", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "Suppose you are given three points (not collinear) and a line (that does \
not contain any of the points), and suppose you want to map this structure by \
a projective transformation to another of the same kind.  ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["Is it possible?",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Solution.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Well, the three points form a ",
  StyleBox["triangle",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and the given line is ",
  StyleBox["not concurrent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with any two of the sides of the triangle.  So the four lines (the given \
line plus the three sides of the triangle) form a ",
  StyleBox["non-degenerate quadrilateral",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  This figure can be mapped to any other non-degenerate quadrilateral, \
say, one determined by three other points (not collinear) and another line \
(that does not contain any of the points).  Since  projective transformations \
preserve meets of lines, the first triple of points will be moved to the \
second triple, and the first special line will be moved to the second. ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.F.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
We work out a simple example starting with a standard triangle and \
line.  \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(M = 
      MQL[Join[PES[{u, v, w}], {x + y + z}], 
        Join[PES[{u, v, w}], {x - y + z}]]; \nM // MatrixForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[
      RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
            {"1", "0", "0"},
            {"0", \(-1\), "0"},
            {"0", "0", "1"}
            }], ")"}],
      (MatrixForm[ #]&)]], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(TP[M]/@{u, v, w}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({u, \(-v\), w}\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["TP[M]@(a u + b v + c w)", "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a\ u - b\ v + c\ w\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["TL[M]@(a x + b y + c z)", "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a\ x - b\ y + c\ z\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell["Here is another example.", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(M = 
      MQL[Join[PES[{u, v, w}], {x + y + z}], 
        Join[PES[{u, v, u + v + w}], {x - y + z}]]; \nM // MatrixForm\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[
      RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
            {"1", "0", "0"},
            {"0", \(-1\), "0"},
            {"1", "1", "1"}
            }], ")"}],
      (MatrixForm[ #]&)]], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(TP[M]/@{u, v, w}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({u, \(-v\), u + v + w}\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(TP[M]\)[a\ u + b\ v + c\ w] // Tidy\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((a + c)\)\ u + \((\(-b\) + c)\)\ v + c\ w\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(TL[M]\)[x + y + z] // Tidy\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(x - y + z\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(TL[M]\)[a\ x + b\ y + c\ z] // Tidy\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a\ x - b\ y + \((\(-a\) + b + c)\)\ z\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell["Now here is a possible degenerate case.", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "We want to map three points and a line to another such figure.  Suppose, \
however, the line passes through one of the points but not through two of the \
points.  In this case the quadrilateral is degenerate.  Are there \
transformations not equivalent to the identity which leave this figure ",
  StyleBox["fixed",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Solution.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Yes!  Suppose the three points are ",
  StyleBox["u", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["v", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and ",
  StyleBox["w", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".   The sides of the triangle are therefore ",
  StyleBox["z", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["x", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["y", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  A line passing through ",
  StyleBox["w", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " will have the form ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ x + b\ y\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "and it intersects the opposite side ",
  StyleBox["z", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " of the triangle in the point represented by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(b\ u - a\ v\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Call that point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["P",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If we suppose the line does not pass either through ",
  StyleBox["u", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " or ",
  StyleBox["v", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", then we can suppose neither of ",
  StyleBox["a", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " or ",
  StyleBox["b", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is zero.  There are many lines passing through ",
  StyleBox["P", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " different from the given line and different from ",
  StyleBox["z", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Indeed the lines through ",
  StyleBox["P", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " all have the form  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ c\ x + b\ c\ y + d\ z\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Two distinct lines both different from the given one and from ",
  StyleBox["z", "InlineInput"],
  " are:\n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ x + b\ y + z\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ x + b\ y - z\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Taking the sides of the triangle and ",
  StyleBox["one",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of these lines forms a non-degenerate quadrilateral.  This can be moved \
to the other quadrilateral obtained by using the other line.  This \
transformation will leave the ",
  StyleBox["original triangle fixed",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " as well as the point ",
  StyleBox["P", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  It therefore leaves the line ",
  StyleBox["z", "InlineInput",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " pointwise fixed and it leaves the line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ x + b\ y\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " fixed.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.F.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["We now do the calculations to demonstrate such a case:", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(M = MQL[{z, x, y, a\ x + b\ y + z}, {z, x, y, a\ x + b\ y - z}]; \n
    M // MatrixForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    TagBox[
      RowBox[{"(", GridBox[{
            {"1", "0", "0"},
            {"0", "1", "0"},
            {"0", "0", \(-1\)}
            }], ")"}],
      (MatrixForm[ #]&)]], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(TP[M]/@{u, v, w, b\ u - a\ v, u + v + w}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({u, v, \(-w\), b\ u - a\ v, u + v - w}\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Thus, the point ",
  StyleBox["u+v+w", "InlineInput"],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " left fixed."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(TL[M]\)[a\ x + b\ y]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a\ x + b\ y\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PE[a\ x + b\ y, z]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(b\ u - a\ v\)], "Output"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
},
